    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Understood. Let's refine the explanation with a focus on maintaining a professional tone while still acknowledging the unique nature of the 'residuals' concept.\n\nIn the context of the NDA, 'residuals' refer to the knowledge and skills that you may inadvertently retain after working with confidential information. These are not the tangible data itself but rather the insights and techniques you've acquired. For instance, if you learn a novel method for optimizing database queries while working with your new client, this knowledge could be considered a 'residual.' You are generally free to apply this knowledge to your other clients, as long as you do not disclose any specific confidential information that was shared with your new client.\n\nIt's important to distinguish between the residual knowledge you've gained through your own experience and the actual confidential data. Think of it as the difference between a chef who has learned a new recipe and the recipe itself. The chef can use the recipe to create dishes for other clients, but they must not reveal the original recipe's secrets. In your case, you can use the optimization techniques you've learned, but you must ensure that you do not share any proprietary information that was provided by your new client.\n\nTo avoid any potential issues, it's always a good practice to document your work and",
      "source": "target",
